description:
A trace has recorded every memory write a program made, with the instruction that made it. Answer the questions of lesson 3.03:

```cpp
struct Write { std::uint64_t rip; std::uint64_t address; std::uint32_t size; std::string operand; };

std::vector<std::uint64_t> whatWrites(const std::vector<Write>& trace, std::uint64_t address, std::uint32_t size);
std::optional<std::int64_t> displacementOf(const std::string& operand);
std::vector<std::uint64_t> objectCandidates(const std::vector<Write>& trace, std::uint64_t address);
```

- **whatWrites** lists the instructions (`rip`) whose write overlaps the bytes `address` to `address + size - 1`, each once, in the order they first appear in the trace.
- **displacementOf** reads the displacement from a memory operand in disassembler style: `[rbx+0x1C]` gives 0x1C, `[rax-0x8]` gives -8, `[rbx]` gives 0, `[rax+rcx*4+0x10]` gives 0x10. Numbers are `0x` hex. If there is no `[`...`]` it returns `std::nullopt`.
- **objectCandidates** takes every write that covers exactly `address` (starts at it) and works out the object's start as `address - displacement`; it returns the distinct results in increasing order. Operands with an index register (a `*`) are skipped, because their displacement is not a member offset.

